# The two things the client says about itself, rather than about whatever screen is up.
#
# Everything else on the panel belongs to something: a row describes a node, a card describes
# the link, the app bar names the screen it heads. "A newer release is out there" and "something
# is in flight" are true of the client, on whichever tab you happen to be looking at - and until
# these two existed the first was visible only inside Settings > About and the second was not
# visible anywhere.
#
# The split between them is the whole design: the bar is for what is *moving* and costs no row,
# the banner is for what has *settled* and costs the rows it takes.

scene demo
scale 4
delay 220

tab nodes
hold 800

# Moving. The radio is answering the config handshake, which is a request already sent - the
# difference between "this is working" and "this is stuck", and the state that made every
# Settings section say "not loaded" with nothing to say why. Watch the hairline under the tabs:
# the list underneath does not move, because the bar hangs in the gap the tab strip already
# leaves rather than taking a row.
syncing on
hold 1600
syncing off
hold 600

# Settled. A check has come back with something newer, and it stays said until it is installed -
# so it is worth rows, and it takes them. Every screen carries it, which is the point: the one
# screen that already knew was the only screen that said so.
update available
hold 1400

# It follows you across the tabs, because it is not about any of them.
tab status
hold 1000
tab messages
hold 1000

# And it stands down on the one screen that states the same thing in more detail. A banner
# pointing at a section is noise on that section - the same rule that keeps the app bar's
# overline from repeating the tab name.
tab settings
key a
hold 1400
key b
hold 600

# The other settled state: installed, waiting for a restart. A finished job rather than an
# outstanding one, which is a different family and reads as one without being read.
update ready
hold 1600
